  "decline_reason": "",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Under section 43, rejection is mandatory where a connotation of the mark, or a sign within it, makes use for the claimed goods or services likely to deceive or cause confusion.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s43",
          "quote": "An application for the registration of a trade mark in respect of particular goods or services must be rejected if, because of some connotation that the trade mark or a sign contained in the trade mark has, the use of the trade mark in relation to those goods or services would be likely to deceive or cause confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual says that the mere presence of a place name does not automatically establish a connotation sufficient to trigger section 43.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/3#1~1",
          "quote": "However, the mere presence within a trade mark of a person's name, a descriptive term, a geographical place name or a reference to the size of packages or intended use of a product does not automatically demonstrate a connotation sufficient to trigger section 43."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "For a standalone geographical reference, the Manual identifies three cumulative criteria for confusion: consumers have reason to associate the goods or services with the place, that association would influence an average consumer's purchasing decision, and no other elements or surrounding circumstances mitigate the risk.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/9/9/3~1",
          "quote": "A trade mark containing a standalone geographical reference will cause confusion if each of the following criteria are met: Consumers would have reason to associate the goods/services with the geographical location; The association between the place and the goods/services would influence the purchasing decision of an average consumer of the goods/services. There are no other elements in the mark, or surrounding circumstances, which would mitigate the risk of confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Qualifying words such as “made in” are unnecessary: the Manual says an unqualified geographical reference may be likely to cause confusion even without deceiving consumers.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/9/9/3~1",
          "quote": "Qualifiers (e.g. “made in”, “product of”) are not necessary for a connotation to be present in a mark. A geographical reference used without any qualifying information may not result in consumers being deceived, but may be likely to cause confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Where the mark connotes geographical origin, the Manual requires consideration of whether the specification includes goods or services from elsewhere when consumers would reasonably expect them to originate from the named place.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/9#1",
          "quote": "If a trade mark containing a geographical reference is found to connote geographical origin, consideration must then be given to whether ordinary consumers would be deceived or confused as a result. This includes assessing whether the scope of the specification would include goods/services produced in areas outside the geographical location when a consumer would reasonably expect them to originate from the location referenced in the trade mark."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual's threshold is a real and tangible danger of confusion, not a mere possibility; it does not require confusion to be more probable than not.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/3/3/1~1",
          "quote": "There must be a real and obvious danger of the buying public being deceived and/or confused by the secondary meaning within the trade mark. A possibility of confusion or a suggestion that deception might occur is not sufficient."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/3/3/1~1",
          "quote": "the use of the word “likely” in this context does not import a requirement that it be more probable than not that the mark has that effect. The probability of deception or confusion must be finite and non-trivial. There must be a “real tangible danger of its occurring”."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The surrounding circumstances relevant under the Manual include how the goods or services are purchased, the physical characteristics of goods and other factors affecting consumer perceptions.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/9/9/3~1",
          "quote": "Surrounding circumstances include the manner in which the goods/services are purchased, the physical characteristics of goods and any other factor which would affect consumer perceptions."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual says deception or confusion is unlikely where the geographical reference is ambiguous or fanciful, or the average consumer would not perceive the word as a geographical reference.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/9#1",
          "quote": "Deception or confusion is not likely to occur if the geographic reference is ambiguous or fanciful (FORGED IN THE OUTBACK on Vehicles, ANTARCTIC BERRIES for Frozen strawberries) or when a word will not be seen as a geographical reference in the eyes of an average consumer (MOCHA for confectionery)."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    }
  ],
  "declined": false,
